  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nAfter another full round of persistence weather Saturday, the \npattern upstream of TX begins to push a weather change over our \nlocal area. Light winds and dry air will make for another pleasant \nevening for Saturday, but by Sunday morning the return of onshore \nwinds will send the dew point and RH values upward. Model consensus \nhas the winds blowing mostly SE Sunday, but the moisture loading \nwill probably take a day before we get back into that sticky \ntropical heat. So while the impacts will begin Sunday, they may not \nbe too noticeable until Monday with warmer min temps and higher heat \nindex values in the afternoon. \n\nUnfortunately the consensus trends have a strengthening subtropical \nridge over TX to slow the progress of the return flow and the \ndeveloping upper troughing pattern over the Pacific. What looked \nlike some opportunity for some streamer convection for next week by \nprevious packages, the later model runs are coming in drier and \ndrier. Thus it's beginning to look like September all over again \npattern-wise where the models project a trough to arrive about 9 \ndays out only to push the pattern change backwards to delay the \ndrought relief. Heat index values now look favorable to top 100 \ndegrees over many areas for at least the first half of next week.",
